# runloop_msg_queue_push() defers an off-main caller before touching
# anything: the worker's message waits under the message queue lock
# for the main thread's iterate to replay it. Its widget, font,
# accessibility and settings work therefore happens on the main
# thread for every caller.
@boundary runloop_msg_queue_push

# The shader backends' per-frame sensor uniforms: the read side of a
# seqlock whose only writer is input_driver_poll() on the main thread
# (nine floats published as bits behind sensor_snap_seq). The getter
# call inside the helper is how it reaches the atomics, not a live
# state read.
input_driver_read_sensor_snapshot|input_state_get_ptr
# Release-store of the atomic shader-demand latch (shader_uses_sensors),
# read with acquire by the poll's demand check. A latch write, not a
# state read.
input_driver_set_shader_uses_sensors|input_state_get_ptr
# video_thread_swap_count: a documented cross-thread accessor. The
# video thread's own writes to swap_count happen under thr->lock in
# the wrapper's frame handler; this reader returns the field directly
# when called on the video thread (or with no wrapper), and takes the
# same thr->lock otherwise. The getter call is how it reaches the
# field; both sides are ordered by the lock.
video_thread_swap_count|video_state_get_ptr

# The widgets/menu/font/display-text stack, verified for the
# frame-context roots. Two safety arguments cover all five pairs:
#
# Batch state: gfx_display's quad batch (batch_* fields) is filled,
# flushed and latched only on the thread that renders - the video
# thread under the wrapper - so the flush/latch calls these callees
# make through disp_get_ptr touch thread-confined state. The
# main-written scalars on the same struct (framebuf dims, header
# height) are not read by these callees; menu drivers take their
# dimensions from their own snapshots per the menu sweep.
font_driver_render_msg|disp_get_ptr
menu_driver_frame|disp_get_ptr
gfx_widgets_frame|disp_get_ptr
#
# Vtable dispatch: the video_state reads are current_video, poke and
# data - the driver call pointers - which change only at video
# init/free, and a frame cannot execute across either (the wrapper
# joins and frees through blocking commands). Reading them to
# dispatch is reading init-stable pointers.
gfx_widgets_frame|video_state_get_ptr
gfx_display_draw_text|video_state_get_ptr

# The same batch and vtable arguments as the five pairs above, reached
# from the frame roots themselves rather than through the menu and
# widgets stack. All five touch gfx_display_flush_as - the batch_*
# fields - and past it only blend_on, which is the same render-thread
# bookkeeping, and dispctx, which changes at display-driver init.
# Keyed on the reading function, so one line covers gl2_frame,
# gl3_frame and vulkan_frame alike.
gfx_display_blend_begin|disp_get_ptr
gfx_display_blend_end|disp_get_ptr
gfx_display_draw|disp_get_ptr
gfx_display_scissor_begin|disp_get_ptr
gfx_display_draw_text|disp_get_ptr

# video_thread_hw_allowed() decides whether a hardware core may run
# under the wrapper, from the core's context type and the configured
# video driver's name. Both are settled before the video thread exists:
# the context type is the core's, taken at content load, and changing
# the driver goes through a reinit that joins and frees the thread. The
# settings pointer itself is stable for the process (config_st is
# assigned only at init and deinit).
#
# The one transient: the menu writes arrays.video_driver when the driver
# is picked, before the reinit it queues, so a frame in that window can
# compare against a name mid-copy. It is bounded - the comparison is
# against a fixed literal in a fixed-size array that already held a
# terminator, so it reads no further than the literal's length - and the
# reinit that follows settles it. Latching instead is not free: the
# macro that calls this is evaluated from inside gl2 and gl3 init,
# before the active driver or a latch would be set.
video_thread_hw_allowed|config_get_ptr
video_thread_hw_allowed|video_state_get_ptr

# A note on the counts: how many entries this audits depends on how wide
# the build is, because an entry it cannot see in the binary is not a
# finding. A default Linux build audits about 42 of them; Vulkan, Qt,
# ffmpeg, HID and Bluetooth take it to 59, which is what surfaced the
# five gfx_display pairs and the two above, and the audio device
# callbacks with JACK, PipeWire and PulseAudio built take it to 76. A
# narrow build reporting no findings is not the same as a clean tree.
#
# The Windows entries are reachable too, which is worth knowing because
# they are a good part of what a Linux build cannot see - WASAPI, WDM-KS,
# DirectSound, XAudio, ASIO, raw input, DirectInput enumeration, the
# modeline resync and the D3D11 and D3D12 frame contexts:
#
#   ./configure --host=x86_64-w64-mingw32 --disable-qt && make
#   tools/thread_read_audit.py --binary ./retroarch.exe \
#      --objdump x86_64-w64-mingw32-objdump
#
# That audits 62, also clean. Between the two, 27 entries are left that
# neither build contains - Apple, Android, emscripten, the consoles,
# Steam, and a few drivers whose library is not packaged. Those need
# their own toolchain; --list-unaudited names them for whichever binary
# it is given.
